      {
        "id": "p8-q40-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that the remainder of $P_1(x)P_2(x)$ when divided by $x^2+1$ is $(bd-ac)+(ad+bc)x$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": {
            "format": "maths-editor-document-v1",
            "version": 1,
            "blocks": [
              {
                "id": "p8-q40-a-short-result",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "R(x)=(bd-ac)+(ad+bc)x",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              },
              {
                "id": "p8-q40-a-short-method",
                "type": "paragraph",
                "fontSize": null,
                "align": "left",
                "spaceBefore": 0,
                "spaceAfter": 2,
                "lineHeight": 1.4,
                "indent": 0,
                "inlines": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Multiply the two division identities; all terms except the product of the remainders contain ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2+1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": " as a factor. Divide the remaining product by ",
                    "marks": []
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "math",
                    "latex": "x^2+1",
                    "display": false
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ".",
                    "marks": []
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          "worked": "Write the division transformations as\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP_1(x)&=(x^2+1)Q_1(x)+ax+b\\\\\nP_2(x)&=(x^2+1)Q_2(x)+cx+d.\n\\end{align*}$$\nFor the polynomial\n$$H(x)=(x^2+1)Q_1(x)Q_2(x)+Q_1(x)(cx+d)+Q_2(x)(ax+b),$$\nmultiplication gives\n$$\\begin{align*}\nP_1(x)P_2(x)&=(x^2+1)H(x)+(ax+b)(cx+d)\\\\\nP_1(x)P_2(x)&=(x^2+1)H(x)+acx^2+(ad+bc)x+bd\\\\\nP_1(x)P_2(x)&=(x^2+1)(H(x)+ac)+(ad+bc)x+bd-ac.\n\\end{align*}$$\nThe final term has degree at most $1$, less than the divisor's degree. Therefore the remainder is $(bd-ac)+(ad+bc)x$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 92
      },
      {
        "id": "p8-q40-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Expand $(ax+b)(cx+d)$ and substitute $x^2$ with the number $-1$. What do you notice?",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(bd-ac)+(ad+bc)x$, the same as the remainder in part (a).",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}\n(ax+b)(cx+d)&=acx^2+adx+bcx+bd\\\\\n(ax+b)(cx+d)&=acx^2+(ad+bc)x+bd.\n\\end{align*}$$\nReplacing $x^2$ by $-1$ gives\n$$-ac+(ad+bc)x+bd=(bd-ac)+(ad+bc)x.$$\nThis is the remainder found in part (a).",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 44
      }
    ]
